WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00.360 --> 00:02.980
It’s not only what you say, it’s how you say it.

2
00:03.850 --> 00:09.250
We will explore the art of delivery and give you tips and techniques that you can use in a variety of settings.

3
00:09.830 --> 00:16.842
It is so critical to make sure that you’ve done the hard work around structuring your message, around creating a clear and compelling storyline.

4
00:16.842 --> 00:20.299
It would be a shame to have it all fall down at the moment of delivery.

5
00:20.299 --> 00:27.360
There are a lot of different ways that you can inject energy into your delivery, but also ensure that you’re going to keep your audience engaged,

6
00:27.850 --> 00:30.630
and they will walk away knowing the most important things.

7
00:31.110 --> 00:41.250
And that’s why we want to explore the different elements of vocal delivery, your physical presence, and things that you can use in a variety of settings to ensure that the way you tell your story,

8
00:41.730 --> 00:51.650
the way you deliver your message, is just as impactful as the content of that message. One of the videos I love watching is the Will Stephen TEDx address,

9
00:52.070 --> 00:57.510
where he actually talks about absolutely nothing as he’s delivering a TEDx talk,

10
00:57.890 --> 01:08.740
but his vocal delivery, his physical presence, is so engaging, he creates a real emotional connection with the audience. And meanwhile, he’s talking absolute gibberish.

11
01:09.410 --> 01:17.730
But what it does do is it drives home the idea of how you can divorce the content of your speech or your presentation

12
01:18.180 --> 01:23.300
and really see how the vocal, physical techniques can come to life.

13
01:23.750 --> 01:32.930
There are a lot of techniques of what you can do with your voice, whether that’s intonation, emphasis, speaking more softly, drawing your audience in.

14
01:33.510 --> 01:36.230
We will explore that with one of our experts a little later.

15
01:36.810 --> 01:42.309
And in terms of physical presence, again, you may have seen things about the power pose or what have you,

16
01:42.309 --> 01:51.580
but there are ways that in your physical presence, whether you’re in a small group setting or a large group setting, you can use and deploy physical presence in a different way.

17
01:51.780 --> 01:57.830
And in terms of creating an emotional connection with your audience, this is really going into the element of conversation.

18
01:58.310 --> 02:05.950
So imagining that, whether you’re just with one person or a room full of people, how do you create a sense of emotional connection with them?

